In physical science, an inverse-square law is any scientific law stating that the observed "intensity" of a specified physical quantity (being nothing more than the value of the physical quantity) is inversely proportional to the square of the the distance from the source of that physical quantity. 
The fundamental cause for this can be understood as geometric dilution corresponding to point-source radiation into three-dimensional space. 

